WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.000
Ten is one more than nine.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.880
A hundred is one more than ninety-nine.

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:06.004
A thousand is one more than nine

00:00:06.004 --> 00:00:06.390
hundred and ninety-nine.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.090
Every place value is one more than a multiple of nine.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.250
That is why digit sums work.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.080
This gives a test for multiples of nine, and of three,

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.950
that takes seconds, even for enormous numbers.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.890
It also survives when digits are rearranged.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:26.770
Is four thousand seven hundred and twenty-five a

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:27.590
multiple of nine?

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:29.240
Pause and add its digits.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:36.140
Each thousand leaves one over after removing nines.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:38.300
So four thousands leave four.

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:39.980
Seven hundreds leave seven.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:41.560
Two tens leave two.

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:43.220
The five units stay.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:46.180
What is left over is exactly the digit sum.

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:50.240
Four plus seven plus two plus five is eighteen.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:54.430
Eighteen is a multiple of nine, so the number is too.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:57.390
In general, a number and its digit sum leave the same

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.360
remainder when divided by nine.

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:03.530
The digit sum lets us find the remainder, too.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.820
Five hundred and eighty-three has digit sum sixteen.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:09.070
Remove as many complete groups of nine from sixteen

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:09.880
as you can.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.240
Sixteen is seven more than nine.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.550
So five hundred and eighty-three leaves remainder seven

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:17.120
when divided by nine.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:20.230
Rearranging digits does not change their sum.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.760
So every arrangement of the digits one, two and six is a

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:24.770
multiple of nine.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.350
And since nine is a multiple of three,

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.170
the same digit-sum test works for three.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:32.650
It solves missing digits too.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.390
Five, blank, eight is a multiple of nine.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.880
The known digits add to thirteen, so the blank must be

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.350
five, making eighteen.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:45.310
A number and its digit sum have the same remainder on

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:48.120
division by nine, and on division by three.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:52.140
Notebook challenge: what digit makes seven, three,

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:54.840
blank, four a multiple of nine?
